---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const links = [
  { label: 'Try FastBreak', href: 'https://fastbreak-eight.vercel.app' },
  { label: 'Code, test set and results on GitHub', href: 'https://github.com/rama-kata/FastBreak' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Building FastBreak"
  description="How I built an NBA stats assistant that shows the query and rows behind every answer, and what measuring it honestly taught me."
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-2xl py-20 sm:py-28" aria-labelledby="post-title">
    <header class="text-center">
      <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-[var(--color-muted-foreground)] dark:text-[var(--color-muted-foreground-on-dark)]">
        November 2026 · Project write-up
      </p>
      <h1 id="post-title" class="mt-6 text-5xl font-semibold leading-[0.98] tracking-tight sm:text-6xl">
        Building FastBreak
        <span class="font-display block italic text-[0.9em] text-[var(--color-muted-foreground)] dark:text-[var(--color-muted-foreground-on-dark)]">
          an NBA stats assistant that shows its work
        </span>
      </h1>
      <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-x-6 gap-y-2 text-sm font-medium">
        {links.map((l) => <a href={l.href} rel="noopener noreferrer">{l.label} ›</a>)}
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="post mt-16">
      <p>
        FastBreak answers plain-English questions about the NBA, like "What was the Warriors' regular season
        record in 2015-16?" or "Who has the most 20-point games off the bench?", from a database of every game
        since 2015-16 that updates every night. Under every answer it shows the SQL query it ran and the rows
        that query returned, so you never have to take its word for anything.
      </p>
      <p>
        I wanted to build something where I couldn't hide behind a reply that sounds right. A stats question
        has one correct answer, so every claim the system makes can be checked, and that meant I had to
        measure it properly instead of trying it a few times and deciding it worked.
      </p>

      <figure>
        <img src="/writing/fastbreak/answer.png" alt="FastBreak answering the Warriors' 2015-16 record, with the number 73 circled in the headline and in the result table, and the SQL query beside it" loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>Every answer is printed like a box score. Point at a number in the answer and it gets circled in the rows it came from.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>How it works</h2>
      <p>
        The data comes from NBA.com through the nba_api library. Loading every season's game log took about
        100 seconds: 14,128 games and 300,879 player rows. Who started each game is only in the box scores,
        though, and that is about 14,000 separate requests, so that part ran overnight and had to survive
        NBA.com blocking me partway through. Each game is recorded as it loads, so a crash or a block just
        means the next run picks up where the last one stopped. A nightly job at 9am adds the new games and
        reloads the current season, because the NBA corrects stats after the fact.
      </p>
      <p>
        The assistant itself is deliberately simple. A language model, qwen3:14b running on Ollama on my home
        PC with two graphics cards, writes a SQL query for the question. The query runs on a read-only
        connection with a 10 second limit. Then the model writes the answer from the rows, and a check holds
        the answer back if any number in it isn't in those rows. The web app is Next.js on Vercel, talking to
        an API on my PC through a Tailscale tunnel.
      </p>

      <h2>What I found</h2>

      <h3>Score the rows, not the reply</h3>
      <p>
        Before building the assistant, I wrote 50 test questions with the SQL that answers them, and saved the
        rows each one returns. An answer only counts when the assistant's query returns the same rows. Reading
        replies and judging them would have been faster, and it would have hidden most of what I found below.
        I drafted the questions with Claude Code. I checked 12 of the answers against outside sources myself,
        and approved the other 38 on Claude Code's source checks, with each link written down next to the
        question.
      </p>

      <h3>Half the wrong answers had the right numbers</h3>
      <p>
        The first version got 20 of 50 right. When I went through the 25 wrong answers, 13 of them had the
        right values with the wrong labels: a team ID instead of the team, seconds instead of minutes, a game
        ID instead of the date. I added rules for how a result should look, which a real app needs anyway to
        display an answer, and the score went to 31 of 50. Comparing question by question, 12 got fixed and 1
        broke, which is very unlikely to be luck.
      </p>

      <h3>Some wrong answers were my fault</h3>
      <p>
        Two of the answers I'd marked wrong were fair readings of my own questions. "The most points scored off
        the bench in a single game" can mean one player or a whole team's bench, and the model picked the team.
        I reworded both questions instead of pretending the model was wrong.
      </p>

      <h3>A setting was quietly deleting my prompt</h3>
      <p>
        Thinking mode, where the model reasons before it answers, roughly doubled the score on hard questions,
        from 5 of 15 to 11 of 15. But its replies ran past 9,000 tokens, and Ollama was using its default
        4,096-token context. Past that limit it silently drops the oldest text, which was the database schema
        at the top of my prompt. Nothing errored. I fixed the context size, capped how long a reply can be, and
        re-ran everything. The score came out the same, 44 of 50, but until that re-run I couldn't have said so.
      </p>

      <h3>Accuracy costs time, and you have to choose</h3>
      <p>
        Thinking on gets 44 of 50 right but takes about 55 seconds a question. Thinking off gets 36 of 50 in
        about 6 seconds. I built a router where a quick call decides whether a question is hard, which got 40
        of 50 at about 11 seconds. I also tried a small open model to make the simple questions faster, and it
        got 1 of 50.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then the site went live, and the first hard question I asked on it took 83 seconds. The page sat on its
        first step the whole time, and from the outside it looked broken. So the live site now runs with
        thinking off. It is right less often, but nobody waits a minute and a half wondering if it crashed. That
        was a product decision, not a technical one, and I wrote down why in the repo.
      </p>

      <figure>
        <img src="/writing/fastbreak/working.png" alt="FastBreak working on a question, with a timestamped list of steps and a Stop button" loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>While it works, the steps show up with timestamps, so a slow answer looks slow instead of stuck.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h3>The check can't catch the wrong question</h3>
      <p>
        One of the first questions on the live site was "Who scored the most points in a game last season?",
        and it answered "Shai
        Gilgeous-Alexander, with 2531 points." That is his whole season. The query added up every game instead
        of taking the best one, and my number check passed, because 2531 really was in the rows. The check
        proves every number came from the data. It can't tell you the query answered a different question. That
        is exactly why the query and the rows are always on screen, and the miss is now in my test set. When I
        asked it again, it got it right (Bam Adebayo's 83), which is its own lesson: the model doesn't make the
        same mistake every time.
      </p>

      <h3>50 questions is a small test</h3>
      <p>
        36 of 50 sounds precise, but with 50 questions the true rate is anywhere from about 58% to 82%. One
        question moves the score by 2 points, and a re-run of the same setup flipped 3 questions each way. So I
        compare runs question by question instead of by the headline number, and the site says "Right on 36 of
        50 test questions" rather than a percentage that sounds more certain than it is.
      </p>

      <h3>The data fails in ways you only see live</h3>
      <p>
        On the first night of the season, the nightly job recorded a game as failed because it was still in the
        first quarter. A week later the job crashed, because my PC had been off at 9am, so it ran in the evening
        while games were being played and NBA.com sent back a box score with no team stats yet. Each of these is
        written up in the repo with what happened, why, the fix, and the test that now catches it.
      </p>

      <h2>What I learnt</h2>
      <p>
        The main lesson was the same one cellar taught me, but sharper: decide how you'll measure something
        before you build it, and make the measure strict. Scoring by rows instead of by how a reply reads is
        what found the label problems, my own bad questions, and the context bug. The second lesson was to be
        honest about what a check can and can't do. "Every number is checked" is true, and it still let a
        confidently wrong answer through. The third was that accuracy, speed and how something feels to use pull
        against each other, and choosing between them is part of the job, not something the numbers decide for
        you.
      </p>
      <p>
        I built this with Claude Code again, including the redesign of the web app, where every answer is laid
        out like a newspaper box score. The useful part wasn't the speed. It was asking it to show me the
        evidence for a result, and then checking that evidence myself before I believed it.
      </p>

      <figure>
        <img src="/writing/fastbreak/home.png" alt="The FastBreak home page with last night's scores across the top and the headline 'Ask anything about the NBA. See the proof.'" loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>The home page, with last night's games across the top and the tested accuracy under the question box.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>Where it stands</h2>
      <p>
        FastBreak is live and answers from my PC whenever it's on. It gets 36 of 50 test questions right with
        thinking off, in about 6 seconds. Next I want to grow the test set to 150 or 200 questions, mostly from
        what real people ask, so the score means more, and run each setup more than once. I also want to see
        whether fine-tuning a small model on this database can match the big one on simple questions, the way it
        did for cellar.
      </p>
      <p>
        You can <a href="https://fastbreak-eight.vercel.app" rel="noopener noreferrer">try it</a> while my machine
        is on, and the code, the test set and every run's results are
        <a href="https://github.com/rama-kata/FastBreak" rel="noopener noreferrer">on GitHub</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .post {
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    line-height: 1.75;
    color: var(--color-foreground);
  }
  :global(html.dark) .post { color: var(--color-background); }
  .post p { margin: 0 0 1.4em; }
  .post h2 {
    font-family: 'Instrument Serif', Georgia, serif;
    font-style: italic;
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: 2.1rem;
    line-height: 1.15;
    margin: 2.4em 0 0.7em;
  }
  .post h3 {
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    margin: 2em 0 0.5em;
  }
  .post figure { margin: 2.5em 0; }
  .post img {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border-radius: 1rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }
  :global(html.dark) .post img { border-color: var(--color-border-on-dark); }
  .post figcaption {
    margin-top: 0.75em;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    text-align: center;
    color: var(--color-muted-foreground);
  }
  :global(html.dark) .post figcaption { color: var(--color-muted-foreground-on-dark); }
</style>
